00:00:08.139 --> 00:00:13.389
If you’re watching this video you’re probably
familiar with the story of the disasters and

00:00:13.389 --> 00:00:19.000
also comical battle of Karansebes as it is
by far my most popular video to date.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:23.810
However I ended the video on note of, we don’t
know what exactly happened, because quite

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:28.990
frankly, I did not have the time to go on
a rabbit hole hunt for some maybe nonexistent

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:30.949
primary sources about the battle.

00:00:30.949 --> 00:00:35.790
But now, we’re back here well over a year
from my original video, and I have managed

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:40.660
to find some extra sources about the battle
that may shine some light on the actual events

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:45.190
that happened near Karansebes that September
of 1788.

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:49.420
So the story I presented at the start of my
first video is the main story that is presented

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:52.120
on most websites including Wikipedia.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:57.490
To a certain extent that story is true but
widely over exaggerated.

00:00:57.490 --> 00:01:04.610
For example the ridiculously high ten thousand
casualty count comes from an 1834 French publication,

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:09.790
which most certainly took some liberties to
portray the rival Austrian army as incompetent.

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:14.700
Continuing on from there, we can presume there
where many more second hand publications mostly

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:19.560
from rivaled Austrian countries taking large
liberties with the facts or even making up

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:20.560
new ones.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:25.250
Such as the made up order of an artillery
fire directed straight into an Austrian camp,

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:29.810
or the fact that the Austrian military was
carrying a chest with the entire army’s

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:33.170
payroll, which we know wasn’t an Austrian
military practice.

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:37.560
Suddenly when looking at all of these made
up facts about the battle, it may start to

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:43.120
seem like the events at Karansebes, where
just made up by Austrian rivals as to diminish

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:45.840
their prestige in European politics.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:51.090
However that is very unlikely, as there are
too many articles, from different countries,

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:52.350
talking about the same event.

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:53.350
Which if the battle would have been made up,
would be hard to coordinate between so many

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:54.350
different publications.

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:56.840
This also combined with the fact that it wasn’t
just foreign articles that talked about the

00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:01.280
Battle of Karansebes, and there were Austrian
publications as well, means that it is highly

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:03.920
implausible that the story was made up.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:09.310
In fact, the Karansebes story presented in
my first video, is a centuries long amalgamation

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:14.639
of second hand over exaggerated narratives,
of an event that most likely wasn’t as drastic

00:02:14.639 --> 00:02:17.700
as it is made out to be today.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:23.840
So to get away from this bias and over exaggerations,
records from the actual September of 1788

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:24.930
have to be consulted.

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:30.060
These aren’t as numerous as the second hand
19th century records, but there is enough

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.819
of them to make out what happened in Karansebes.

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:37.200
There are multiple none German or Austrian
sources from 1788, that talk about the Battle

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:42.409
of Karansebes, but they all seem to be reciting
the German and Austrian publications.

00:02:42.409 --> 00:02:47.340
Over all, it seems like there are three main
writings that claim to have first hand sources,

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:50.930
or interviewed first hand sources like the
soldiers from the battle.

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:55.720
One of these writings are the official Ottoman
military accounts, which naturally can’t

00:02:55.720 --> 00:03:00.129
give us a full picture, as they were on the
other side of the war, but can provide valuable

00:03:00.129 --> 00:03:01.319
information.

00:03:01.319 --> 00:03:05.640
Information like the fact that the Ottoman
troops were ready to engage the Austrian army,

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:10.569
only for them to find out in late September,
that the Austrians withdrew from the strategically

00:03:10.569 --> 00:03:15.879
important Timiș-Cerna Gap, of which Karansebes
lies on the northern end of.

00:03:15.879 --> 00:03:19.650
I mentioned this source in my first video
but what I didn’t mention was a different

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:24.309
Ottoman source, that accredits the Austrian
withdrawal, to the Austrian fear of facing

00:03:24.309 --> 00:03:26.360
the terrifying Ottoman army.

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:31.059
However, this is obviously just Ottoman propaganda
at play, but the first source is still sound

00:03:31.059 --> 00:03:35.299
giving us a solid ground to stand on, knowing
that the Austrians did in fact retreat from

00:03:35.299 --> 00:03:37.209
Karansebes for some reason.

00:03:37.209 --> 00:03:42.239
So now we have to look at the German and Austrian
sources claiming to have first hand records.

00:03:42.239 --> 00:03:47.109
The first of these sources, the Politisches
Journal from Hamburg seems like a good place

00:03:47.109 --> 00:03:52.299
to start, as it gives personal testimonies
from witnesses, and sights official Austrian

00:03:52.299 --> 00:03:53.629
court documents.

00:03:53.629 --> 00:03:58.309
It tells us that a skirmish did happen between
the Ottomans and Austrians at some point in

00:03:58.309 --> 00:04:00.590
early September of 1788.

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:04.669
This skirmish most likely occurred somewhere
near the Timiș-Cerna Gap, as it says that

00:04:04.669 --> 00:04:08.939
the “Kaiser ordered a retreat” via Karansebes
towards Temeswar.

00:04:08.939 --> 00:04:14.480
During this confusing retreat, the local Wallachians,
which saw an opportunity to rob the Austrian

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:18.810
soldiers, started to spread rumours throughout
the army, that the Ottomans were in close

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:21.789
pursuit, getting ready to attack at any moment.

00:04:21.789 --> 00:04:27.260
This turned the withdrawal in to a disorderly
panicked mess, during which two Austrian regiments

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:28.640
managed to run in to each other.

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:34.150
Due to the confusion and an expected Ottoman
ambush both regiments mistook each other for

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:36.669
Ottoman soldiers and opened fire.

00:04:36.669 --> 00:04:40.370
They quickly noticed their mistake but the
damage was already done.

00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:43.889
That is where the german source ends this
story, and continues on talking about the

00:04:43.889 --> 00:04:46.569
bigger picture ramifications of the retreat.

00:04:46.569 --> 00:04:50.280
The source doesn't mention if there were any
casualties or deaths but it is most likely

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:52.110
that there were at least a couple.

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:56.440
Going on from there checking the Austrian
article from Real Zeitung, there are some

00:04:56.440 --> 00:04:59.389
similarities with the German source but also
some differences.

00:04:59.389 --> 00:05:04.900
One of the similarities being that the initial
confusion was started by Wallachians, however

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:08.260
the way they started it, does differ from
the German source.

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:12.919
According to the Austrian source the panicked
confusion didn’t happen during the armies

00:05:12.919 --> 00:05:16.720
retreat, but while it was encamped near the
town of Karansebes.

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:21.260
Apparently with the intentions of robbing
the camp, Wallachians started to yell “Turks!

00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:24.840
Turks!”, causing a confusion and fear throughout
the camp.

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:29.639
The Austrian article continues on saying that
during the confusion, two regiments mistook

00:05:29.639 --> 00:05:33.759
each other for Ottoman soldiers, and opened
fire, just as in the German source.

00:05:33.759 --> 00:05:39.000
But the Austrian article does give more explanation
of why the soldiers miss took each other for

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:40.000
Ottomas.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:44.460
Saying that the soldiers opened fire, only
after mishearing a “Halt” command from

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:49.140
one of the opposing regiments officers as
the Ottoman battle cry “Allah!”.

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:54.150
However after a few shots fired, the soldiers
quickly realized their mistake and stopped.

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:59.050
That is where the account ends, and the article
continues on talking about the military actions

00:05:59.050 --> 00:06:02.860
of the Austrian army as they decided to retreat
from Karansebes.

00:06:02.860 --> 00:06:07.750
So even though the Austrian source does differ
a little bit from the German source, the two

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:10.389
tell a pretty similar overarching story.

00:06:10.389 --> 00:06:15.450
Showing us that the main underlying element
of the, “battle”, was a fear of an Ottoman

00:06:15.450 --> 00:06:21.540
attack sparked by local Wallachians, after
which during the confusion two regiments accidentally

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:24.979
opened fire on each other, thinking the other
one was the enemy.

00:06:24.979 --> 00:06:30.380
So even though today, the Battle of Karansebes
is remembered as a drunken scuffle turned

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:34.789
bad that in the end was responsible for ten
thousand casualties.

00:06:34.789 --> 00:06:37.479
The true story is far milder.

00:06:37.479 --> 00:06:42.620
However the truth never stops people from
embellishing the facts, especially when they

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:46.969
have a hidden agenda to push or an entertaining
story to tell.

